  title: "Globalization and the Welfare State",
  subtitle: "A panel data analysis of 32 OECD countries, 1980–2023.",
  heroImage: "/assets/projects/welfare-state-seminar/fig-kof.png",
  status: "Completed · June 2026",
  pdfMeta: "Full text · 40 pages · 1.1 MB",
  contextNote: "Seminar paper in economics at the University of Copenhagen, submitted 3 June 2026 and supervised by Amalie Sofie Jensen. Written with Jonas Skov Nielsen and Sheng Ye Michael Chen. The estimates are correlational, not causal — globalization is not randomly assigned to countries, and that is a limitation we address explicitly rather than write around.",
  executiveSummary: "Does globalization squeeze the welfare state, or does it create demand for more social protection? We examine the question using social security transfers as a share of GDP across 32 OECD countries from 1980 to 2023 — extending Yay and Aksoy (2018) by thirteen years that include both the financial crisis and the pandemic. We find no robust overall relationship. Social globalization is the only one of the four KOF indices significant in the baseline, and that result does not survive a test for reverse causality. What we do find is more interesting: the relationship itself is unstable over time. Rolling regressions show coefficients moving from insignificant to positive and significant as the estimation window fills with post-2008 observations, and a Chow test confirms a structural break at exactly that point. The conclusion is methodological: a single long-run estimate spanning 44 years does not describe one relationship, but the average of several.",
  highlights: [
    { label: "Grade", value: "10" },
    { label: "Method", value: "Panel FE" },
    { label: "Countries", value: "32" },
    { label: "Observations", value: "996" }
  ],
  researchQuestions: [
    "Does the association between globalization and social security transfers, as found in Yay and Aksoy (2018), remain valid when the sample is extended to 2023?",
    "Does the association differ systematically across welfare regimes — or is the regime heterogeneity in the literature an artefact of the period chosen?",
    "Is the relationship stable over time at all, and what does the answer imply for the single coefficients the literature normally reports?"
  ],
  methodology: {
    model: "Two-way fixed effects panel model with country effects δᵢ and year effects γₜ. Social security transfers as a percentage of GDP proxy for the size of the welfare state, and every right-hand-side variable enters with a one-year lag.",
    strategy: "Two specifications. The baseline estimates each KOF index separately with macroeconomic and demographic controls. The heterogeneity specification adds interaction terms between globalization and four welfare regimes, with the social democratic regime as reference, and regime-specific marginal effects are recovered afterwards.",
    data: "32 OECD countries, annual observations 1980–2023, N = 996 in the fully specified models. Dependent variable from CPDS; globalization from KOF; controls from the OECD and the World Bank.",
    estimation: "Within estimator with standard errors clustered by country. Robustness via Driscoll-Kraay standard errors, rolling regressions over 10-year windows, a Chow test for a structural break, and feedback regressions for reverse causality."
  },
  keyFindings: [
    "No robust overall relationship. Of the four KOF indices, only social globalization is significant in the baseline (β = 0.129, t = 2.63). Overall globalization (0.088), economic (−0.020) and political (0.046) are all insignificant under country-clustered standard errors.",
    "The one significant result is likely reverse causality. Feedback regressions show that lagged social security transfers predict social globalization (β = 0.225, t = 2.15) — the opposite direction to the one the literature normally studies.",
    "Regime differences are real but weaker than they look. The Mediterranean regime differs positively from the social democratic one on all four indices, and the liberal regime on overall and economic globalization. But interaction terms measure differences from the reference, not effects in their own right.",
    "Turning to marginal effects within each regime, only one estimate is significant: economic globalization in social democratic welfare states, with a coefficient of −0.187 — the only genuine support for the efficiency hypothesis in the whole analysis.",
    "The relationship is historically contingent. Rolling regressions over 10-year windows show coefficients that are insignificant or negative before 2015 and turn positive and significant in the windows dominated by post-crisis observations (overall globalization: 0.183 in the window ending 2023).",
    "A Chow test rejects parameter stability at 2008 for all four indices (F between 4.9 and 9.9, all p < 0.001). The regime heterogeneity reported by Yay and Aksoy (2018) therefore does not hold once the sample is extended to 2023."
  ],
  robustness: [
    {
      question: "Social globalization is the only significant index. Is that effect real?",
      answer: "Probably not, at least not in that direction. We invert the specification and regress the KOF indices on lagged social security transfers. For overall, economic and political globalization the coefficient is insignificant, but for social globalization it is 0.225 and significant at the 5% level. Decomposing social globalization into its six under-components, only de jure interpersonal globalization is significant (0.355), while its de facto counterpart is 0.044 and insignificant. What drives the association is therefore institutional openness — visa freedom, telecom coverage, international airports — and not realised migration flows. We conclude that the baseline result for social globalization must be read with heavy caution.",
      category: "econometrics"
    },
    {
      question: "The Pesaran CD test rejects cross-sectional independence. So why use country-clustered standard errors?",
      answer: "Because they are the most conservative choice available to us. Country-clustered errors are robust to heteroskedasticity and serial correlation within countries, but not to cross-sectional dependence — which we clearly reject (CD ≈ −3.6, p = 0.000), unsurprising in a panel of 32 open economies sharing business cycles. Driscoll-Kraay standard errors handle exactly that, so we report both. Under Driscoll-Kraay, overall globalization (p = 0.007) and political globalization (p = 0.011) also become significant, and several marginal effects change status. We lead with the conservative errors so that only the most robust coefficients survive, but we are explicit that inference is sensitive to the choice.",
      category: "econometrics"
    },
    {
      question: "Why rolling regressions, when the literature settles for a single coefficient?",
      answer: "That is precisely the reason. One coefficient estimated over 1980–2023 pools periods that do not resemble each other. We re-estimate the baseline on 10-year windows and the pattern is unambiguous: overall, social and political globalization move from insignificant to positive and significant as soon as the window is dominated by post-2008 observations. A Chow test at 2008 rejects parameter stability for all four indices. To our knowledge, rolling regressions have not previously been used in this literature to demonstrate that — likely because the method only becomes informative with a sufficiently long sample. It also explains the disagreement in the field: Yay and Aksoy (2018) report insignificant results with data ending in 2010, which by our windows is before the relationship turns.",
      category: "econometrics"
    },
    {
      question: "Mediterranean and liberal regimes have positive interaction terms. Doesn't that confirm the compensation hypothesis there?",
      answer: "No, and this is an interpretive trap worth being precise about. An interaction term measures the difference relative to the reference category, not the effect within the regime. A significant positive interaction for the Mediterranean regime means it responds differently from social democratic welfare states — not that it expands its welfare state in response to globalization. When we compute marginal effects regime by regime, none of the coefficients for the Mediterranean or liberal regimes are significant. The only regime with a significant effect of its own is the social democratic one, and it is negative. The cross-regime differences are therefore not matched by precisely estimated effects within them.",
      category: "intuition"
    },
    {
      question: "Can any of this be read causally?",
      answer: "No, and this is not a caveat offered out of politeness. Globalization is not randomly assigned, there is no non-globalized control group, and the least globalized economies typically lack the institutional capacity to produce the long data series that could serve as one. The variation behind our estimates is within-country movement in the KOF indices over time, and we interpret it as association. On top of that sits the aggregation problem: transfers as a share of GDP say nothing about benefit quality, eligibility rules, or in-kind services such as healthcare and education. We therefore cannot distinguish changes in the size of the welfare state from changes in the composition of its spending.",
      category: "data"
    }
  ] as const,
  popScience: {
    title: "Does globalization force the welfare state to shrink?",
    text: "It is one of the most persistent claims in economic debate: once capital, firms and labour can move across borders, countries must cut taxes to hold on to them — and then they cannot afford welfare. The opposite claim is just as widespread: open economies are more exposed to external shocks, so citizens demand more social insurance, not less. Both stories are plausible, both have prominent advocates, and they cannot both be right. We tested them on 44 years of data from 32 wealthy countries.",
  },
  popularScienceResult: "Neither holds in general. There simply is no stable relationship between how globalized a country is and how much it spends on social transfers. But the most useful result is not the null itself — it is that the question is posed wrongly. When we looked at ten-year stretches one at a time instead of the whole period at once, the relationship turned out to change sign along the way. Before the financial crisis it points weakly one way; after it, the other. Which means two researchers can study exactly the same question with exactly the same method and reach opposite conclusions, purely because their data stop in different years. That goes a long way towards explaining why the research in this area has disagreed with itself for so long.",
